{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nTeams running several tracker projects against one codebase cannot use the bridge: it is hard-wired\nto one project at three points — `JiraConfig.project` is a single scalar (`_config_schema.py`), the\ninbound fetcher builds its JQL pair from one key and fails closed on an empty one (`fetcher.jqls_for`),\nand the applier aborts a pass when any outbound mutation targets a key outside that project\n(`applier.CrossProjectTargetError`). The only escape is the destructive one-way\n`scripts/retarget_jira_project.py`. Assignee preservation for tracker-unresolvable users is owned by\nin-progress bug `9b94-af9e-4acf-4dff` and taken as a dependency. (A pre-condensation draft sits in an\nepic comment; this description supersedes it.)\n\n## Approach\n\nOne shared ticket store between N repos and N tracker projects; each ticket records the project it\nsyncs into and the repos it belongs to.\n\nThe STORE owns the project set — `.bridge_state/projects.json` on the tickets branch beside\n`bindings.json`/`prev_snapshot.json`. Mechanical reason: `prev_snapshot.json` is last-known-remote\nstate; two repos fetching different project subsets against one shared snapshot would each diff the\nother's projects as remote deletions. A store-owned set removes that failure mode by construction.\n\nBuilt on the provider-neutral `bridge` verb group (epic `2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3`) and the backend-neutral\nport — `RemoteRef(vendor, instance, remote_id)`, `Backend.project`/`Backend.query_project`.\n`Backend.project` is the per-repo default for rebar-created tickets; no new config key.\n\nMigration writes NO events. The ticket project field is tri-state — absent=legacy, explicit-empty=\nnever sync, explicit key=sync there — mirroring `file_impact_scope` (`docs/ticket-model.md`). Absent\nresolves against the legacy default in the mapping, seeded by an idempotent check-then-act ensure\nunit (`docs/migrations.md`; the ordered School-A ledger is explicitly not built). A per-ticket EDIT\nmigration was rejected: four-figure event writes under the store lock, and the MCP-boot ensure entry\nskips under lock contention, so it would silently skip there.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/` — mapping store, fetch fan-out, outbound dispatch, cross-project guard.\n- `src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`, `_store/init.py` — the ensure unit seeding the mapping.\n- `src/rebar/_store/compat.py` — the capability stamp.\n- `src/rebar/reducer/`, `schemas/ticket_state.schema.json`, `_commands/composer.py`, `_lib_writes.py`,\n  `_mcp_writes.py` — the two new ticket fields and their create-time arguments.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/` — the `bridge projects` subcommand.\n- `docs/` — the multi-project model, the mapping, an ADR.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `bridge projects` lists, sets and removes project→repos entries on the store mapping, with the\n      same operations on the library and MCP surfaces; per-surface test asserts each surface reads\n      back an entry written by another.\n- [ ] A store whose mapping holds two projects fetches both in a single pass and each ingested ticket\n      records its source project; reconciler test asserts one JQL pair AND one enrichment pass\n      (parent/comment/issuelink maps) per configured project, both projects' enrichment data present\n      in the merged snapshot, and the recorded project on each created ticket.\n- [ ] A ticket whose project is explicitly empty produces no outbound mutation; differ test asserts an\n      empty mutation list for it while a sibling with a project produces one.\n- [ ] A ticket created with an explicit project targets it rather than the repo default; dispatch test\n      asserts the create call carries the ticket's project.\n- [ ] An outbound update inside the configured set proceeds while one outside it still raises\n      `CrossProjectTargetError`; paired applier test covers both directions.\n- [ ] Every ticket in a pre-feature store resolves to the recorded legacy default with zero events\n      written; migration test asserts byte-identical event count before/after the sweep.\n- [ ] A store whose mapping holds more than one project carries `multi-project-bridge` in\n      `.store-compat.json` `required_capabilities` and a binary lacking it refuses writes, while a\n      single-project store stays writable; store-compat test covers both, including that the\n      stamp and mapping mutation land in one tracker commit (asserted on the commit's tree).\n\n## Child decomposition (disjoint file sets)\n\n- **Project mapping store** — `.bridge_state/projects.json` + helpers + ensure seed. Delivers\n  migration/legacy-default criteria.\n- **`bridge projects` command surface** — subcommand + library/MCP parity per epic `2f79`.\n  Delivers criterion 1.\n- **Ticket project field** — schema, CREATE data, reducer, create-time args on all three surfaces;\n  promote-only rule (cannot change a bound ticket's project). Delivers empty-project criterion.\n- **Ticket repos field** — schema, reducer, create-time arg, inbound defaulting from the mapping.\n- **Multi-project inbound fetch** — `_build_snapshot` (fetcher.py) iterates the configured project\n  set for the base JQL fan-out AND its three enrichment passes — `get_parent_map` /\n  `get_comment_map` / `get_issuelinks_map` called once per project, results merged into the shared\n  snapshot keyed by issue (single-project enrichment would silently drop parent/comment/issuelink\n  data for every project but one); per-query truncation ceiling scoped per project. Delivers the\n  two-project fetch criterion incl. enrichment fidelity.\n- **Multi-project outbound dispatch** — per-ticket project on create; guard relaxed to set\n  membership. Delivers explicit-project + guard criteria.\n- **Store-compat capability stamp** — add `multi-project-bridge` to `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES`; change\n  `_store_compat_unit`'s check-then-act from \"blob absent\" to \"committed `required_capabilities` !=\n  desired set from the mapping\" (today it never re-stamps a converged store); the `bridge projects`\n  set/remove write path writes the re-derived stamp and the mapping mutation in the SAME tracker\n  commit (one atomic publish — no observable intermediate state; a crash before the commit leaves\n  neither write). Ensure sweeps are convergence backstops for any residual divergence, not the\n  trigger. Older binaries cannot create a multi-project mapping at all (they lack the verb), so the\n  atomic-commit writer is the only path that can make a store multi-project; `write_compat_record` computes the\n  capability list; test covers add (>1) and remove (<=1) transitions plus the synchronous-stamp\n  property. Delivers the final criterion.\n- **Docs + ADR** — model, mapping, tri-state field, decision record.\n- **Live multi-project rehearsal** — LAST child: two live Jira Cloud projects on an isolated\n  tickets-branch copy on the encrypted S3 remote (capability from epic `cf3c-1444-863e-49d7`);\n  dry-run-gated, all created issues removed. Fixtures cannot reach the cross-project contamination\n  this validates.\n- **Module-size relief** — files this feature edits sit within ~30 lines of the 800 cap;\n  behaviour-preserving extractions along existing seams, ordered ahead of the story needing room;\n  they deliver no epic criterion.\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nAdditive: absent project = legacy, pre-feature stores behave as today. Single-project stores never\nreceive the stamp, so older binaries keep working; only a genuinely multi-project store fails closed\non them — intended (an older binary would see other projects' tickets as unbound and duplicate\nthem). Rollback per-change on Gerrit; deleting `.store-compat.json` returns every binary to\nimplicit-legacy, the ensure re-stamping on the next covered write.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786618253258886001,"uuid":"bf7de99a-76ee-40b0-a287-8b15634de161"}